<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Reservations.Domain\Reservations.Domain.csproj" />
    <!-- CONVENTIONS.md "Project reference graph": Application -> Domain, BuildingBlocks only.
         Needed for BuildingBlocks.Results.Result<T>/Error — an expected use-case outcome is a
         value, never an exception (CONVENTIONS.md "Errors"). -->
    <PackageReference Include="BuildingBlocks" Version="[0.1.0]" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- GL-34: interactors are internal sealed and registered against the open generic
         IInteractor<,> (CONVENTIONS.md "Use cases" [AT]); the validators beside them are internal
         sealed too. Either way the composition root has to name the concrete types to register
         them, so AddReservationsInfrastructure would not compile without this. Production wiring
         required by "Use cases", not a testing decision: CONVENTIONS.md "Reaching an internal
         from a test" carves out exactly this grant — Application to its own Infrastructure, for
         the composition root — and says in terms that targeting a production assembly is not
         itself a reason. Mirrors GiftLists.Application's and Identity.Application's own identical
         grant. Do not cite this comment to add a production grant of any other shape. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Reservations.Infrastructure" />
    <!-- GL-36: a grant to a test project, so it IS governed by CONVENTIONS.md "Reaching an
         internal from a test"'s ordering (GL-69, which removed the sibling grants in
         Identity.Application and Gateway.Application because they granted nothing; a grant of
         this shape is earned per service, never copied across).

         Route 1, extract a public framework-free type, does not apply: an interactor or
         validator is a use case orchestrating ports, not an algorithm that could be lifted out of
         one. Route 2, resolve through the composition root, cannot serve these tests: resolving
         IInteractor<,> from AddReservationsInfrastructure yields the interactor bound to the
         PRODUCTION adapters (a real IMongoDatabase, a real IBus) and wrapped in the
         Validating/Logging decorators — ReserveGiftInteractorTests constructs the interactor
         against hand-written fakes precisely to avoid all of that (CONVENTIONS.md "Testing").
         Mirrors GiftLists.Application's identical grant to GiftLists.UnitTests exactly. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Reservations.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>

